3620†hypothetical main size L2306:      * if using the cite2162†flex shrink factor : any item that has a cite2359†flex base size smaller than its cite3620†hypothetical main size L2315:     For every unfrozen item on the line, multiply its flex shrink factor by its inner cite2359†flex base size , and note this as its scaled flex shrink factor. Find the ratio of the item’s cite3760†scaled flex shrink factor to the sum of the scaled flex shrink factors of all unfrozen items on the line. Set the item’s cite3757†target main size to its flex base size minus a fraction of the absolute value of the cite3759†remaining free space proportional to the ratio.\nL2316: Note this may result in a negative inner cite162†main size ; it will be corrected in the next step.\nL2317:     4. Fix min/max violations. Clamp each non-frozen item’s cite3757†target main size by its cite3588†used min and max cite162†main sizes and floor its content-box size at zero. If the item’s target main size was made smaller by this, it’s a max violation. If the item’s target main size was made larger by this, it’s a min violation.\nL2318:     5. Freeze over-flexed items. The total violation is the sum of the adjustments from the previous step `∑(clamped size - unclamped size)`. If the total violation is:\nL2319: Zero\nL2320:     Freeze all items.\nL2321: Positive\nL2322:     Freeze all the items with min violations.\nL2323: Negative\nL2324:     Freeze all the items with max violations.\nL2325: \nL2326: Note: This freezes at least one item, ensuring that the loop makes progress and eventually terminates.\nL2327: \nL2328:     6. Return to the start of this loop.\nL2329:   6. Set each item’s used cite162†main size to its cite3757†target main size .\nL2330: Tests\nL2331:   * cite3761†item-with-max-height-and-scrollbar.html†wpt.fyi cite3762†(live test)†wpt.live cite3763†(source)†github.com L2332:   * cite3761†item-with-max-height-and-scrollbar.html†wpt.fyi cite3762†(live test)†wpt.live cite3763†(source)†github.com L2333:   * cite3764†max-width-violation.html†wpt.fyi cite3765†(live test)†wpt.live cite3766†(source)†github.com L2334:   * cite3767†relayout-image-load.html†wpt.fyi cite3768†(live test)†wpt.live cite3769†(source)†github.com L2335:   * cite3770†table-as-item-inflexible-in-column-1.html†wpt.fyi cite3771†(live test)†wpt.live cite3772†(source)†github.com L2336:   * cite3773†table-as-item-inflexible-in-column-2.html†wpt.fyi cite3774†(live test)†wpt.live cite3775†(source)†github.com L2337:   * cite3776†table-as-item-inflexible-in-row-1.html†wpt.fyi cite3777†(live test)†wpt.live cite3778†(source)†github.com L2338:   * cite3779†table-as-item-inflexible-in-row-2.html†wpt.fyi cite3780†(live test)†wpt.live cite3781†(source)†github.com L2339:   * cite3782†total-min-max-violation-zero.html†wpt.fyi cite3783†(live test)†wpt.live cite3784†(source)†github.com L2340: ### 9.8. Definite and Indefinite Sizes\nL2341: \nL2342: Although CSS Sizing cite170†[CSS-SIZING-3] defines cite3785†definite and cite3786†indefinite lengths, Flexbox has several additional cases where a length can be considered definite:\nL2343: \nL2344:   1. If the cite151†flex container has a cite1190†definite cite162†main size , then the post-flexing main sizes of its cite150†flex items are treated as definite.\nL2345: \nL2346:   2. If a cite150†flex item’s cite2197†flex basis is cite1190†definite , then its post-flexing cite162†main size is also definite.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Fragmentation Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-break-3/)\nciteturn1377view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1366view2\",\"pattern\":\"unforced\"}); Total lines: 694\nL53:       5. cite32†3.1.1 Child→Parent Break Propagation L54:     2. cite33†3.2 Breaks Within Boxes: the break-inside property L55:     3. cite34†3.3 Breaks Between Lines: orphans, widows L56:     4. cite35†3.4 Page Break Aliases: the page-break-before, page-break-after, and page-break-inside properties L57:   4. cite36†4 Rules for Breaking L58:     1. cite37†4.1 Possible Break Points L59:     2. cite38†4.2 Types of Breaks L60:     3. cite39†4.3 Forced Breaks L61:     4. cite40†4.4 Unforced Breaks L62:     5. cite41†4.5 Optimizing Unforced Breaks L117: UAs may (but are not required to) adjust the placement of cite101†unforced breaks in parallel formatting contexts to visually balance such side-by-side content, but must not do so to match a cite102†forced break .\nL258: ## 4. Rules for Breaking\nL259: \nL260: A cite79†fragmented flow may be broken across cite71†fragmentainers at a number of cite37†possible break points . In the case of cite39†forced breaks , the UA is required to break the flow at that point. In the case of cite40†unforced breaks , the UA has to choose among the possible breaks that are allowed.\nL261: \nL262: To guarantee progress, fragmentainers are assumed to have a minimum cite203†block size of 1px regardless of their used size.\nL297: When multiple cite223†forced break values apply to a single break point, they combine such that all types of break are honored. When cite118†left , cite119†right , cite120†recto , and/or cite121†verso are combined, the value specified on the latest element in the flow wins.\nL298: \nL299: A forced page break must also occur at a cite222†class A break point if the last line box above this margin and the first one below it do not have the same value for cite225†page . See cite74†[CSS3PAGE] L300: When a forced break occurs, it forces ensuing content into the next fragmentainer of the type associated with the break, breaking through as many fragmentation contexts as necessary until the specified break types are all satisfied. If the forced break is not contained within a matching type of fragmentation context, then the forced break has no effect.\nL301: ### 4.4. Unforced Breaks\nL302: \nL303: While cite26†breaking controls can force breaks, they can also discourage them. An unforced break is one that is inserted automatically by the UA in order to prevent content from overflowing the cite71†fragmentainer . The following rules control whether unforced breaking at a cite37†possible break point is allowed:\nL304: Rule 1\nL305:     A cite79†fragmented flow may break at a cite222†class A break point only if all the cite104†break-after and cite105†break-before values applicable to this break point allow it, which is when at least one of them forces a break or when none of them forbid it (cite124†avoid or cite125†avoid-page /cite126†avoid-column /cite19†avoid-region , depending on the cite38†break type ).\nL306: Rule 2\nL307:     However, if all of them are cite226†auto and a common ancestor of all the elements has a cite103†break-inside value of cite227†avoid , then breaking here is not allowed.\nL308: Rule 3\nL309:     Breaking at a cite228†class B break point is allowed only if the number of line boxes between the break and the start of the enclosing block box is the value of cite107†orphans or more, and the number of line boxes between the break and the end of the box is the value of cite106†widows or more.\nL310: Rule 4\nL311:     Additionally, breaking at cite222†class B or cite229†class C break points is allowed only if the cite103†break-inside property of all ancestors is cite230†auto .\nL312: If the above doesn’t provide enough break points to keep content from overflowing the cite71†fragmentainer , then rule 3 is dropped to provide more break points.\nL313: \nL314: If that still does not lead to sufficient break points, then rules 1, 2 and 4 are dropped in order to find additional breakpoints. In this case the UA may use the avoid s that are in effect at those points to weigh the appropriateness of the new breakpoints; however, this specification does not suggest a precise algorithm.\nL315: If even that does not lead to sufficient break points, cite231†cloned margins/border/padding at the cite232†block-end side are truncated; and if more room is still needed, cite231†cloned margins/border/padding are truncated at the cite233†block-start side as well.\nL316: Finally, if there are no possible break points below the top of the fragmentainer, and not all the content fits, the UA may break anywhere in order to avoid losing content off the edge of the fragmentainer. In such cases, the UA may also fragment the contents of cite206†monolithic elements by slicing the element’s graphical representation. However, the UA must not break at the top of the page, i.e.\nL317: it must place at least some content on each fragmentainer, so that each fragmentainer has a non-zero amount of content, in order to guarantee progress through the content.\nL318: ### 4.5. Optimizing Unforced Breaks\nL319: \nL320: While CSS3 requires that a cite79†fragmented flow must break at allowed break points in order to avoid overflowing the fragmentainers in its fragmentation context, it does not define whether content breaks at a particular cite40†allowed break . However, it is recommended that user agents observe the following guidelines (while recognizing that they are sometimes contradictory):\nL321:   * Break as few times as possible.\nL322:   * Make all fragmentainers that don’t end with a forced break appear to be equally filled with content.\nL323:   * Avoid breaking inside a replaced element.\nL324: \nL325: Suppose, for example, that the style sheet contains orphans : 4, widows : 2, and there is space for 20 lines (line boxes) available at the bottom of the current page, and the next block in normal flow is considered for placement:\nL363: ### 5.2. Adjoining Margins at Breaks\nL364: \nL365: When an unforced break occurs before or after a block-level box, any margins adjoining the break are truncated to zero. When a forced break occurs there, adjoining margins before the break are truncated, but margins after the break are preserved. cite231†Cloned margins are always truncated to zero on block-level boxes.\nL366: \nL367: Note: CSS Fragmentation Level 4 will introduce control over margin truncation at breaks.\nL442: > Applies to: block containers that establish a new inline formatting context\nL443: \nL444:   * Clarified that margins adjoining a Class C break are also truncated in the same way as margins between siblings (Class A). (cite273†Issue 3073†github.com )\nL445: \nL446: > When an unforced break occurs ~~between~~ before or after a block-level box ~~es~~ , any margins adjoining the break ~~truncate to the cite242†remaining fragmentainer extent before the break, and~~ are truncated to zero ~~after the break~~ .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Fonts Module Level 4 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/)\nciteturn1377view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1366view3\",\"pattern\":\"cluster matching\"}); Total lines: 4953\nL86:     7. cite66†4.7 Using named instances from variable fonts: the font-named-instance descriptor L87:     8. cite67†4.8 Font request guidelines L88:       1. cite68†4.8.1 Font loading guidelines L89:       2. cite69†4.8.2 Font fetching requirements L90:     9. cite70†4.9 Controlling Font Display Per Font-Face: the font-display descriptor L91:       1. cite71†4.9.1 Controlling Font Display Per Font-Family via @font-feature-values L92:     10. cite72†4.10 Default font language overriding: the font-language-override descriptor L93:     11. cite73†4.11 Default font metrics overriding: the ascent-override, descent-override and line-gap-override descriptors L94:   5. cite74†5 Font Matching Algorithm L95:     1. cite75†5.1 Localized name matching L96:     2. cite76†5.2 Matching font styles L97:     3. cite77†5.3 Cluster matching L98:     4. cite78†5.4 Character handling issues L99:   6. cite79†6 Font Feature Properties L100:     1. cite80†6.1 Glyph selection and positioning L101:     2. cite81†6.2 Language-specific display L102:     3. cite82†6.3 Kerning: the font-kerning property L103:     4. cite83†6.4 Ligatures: the font-variant-ligatures property L104:     5. cite84†6.5 Subscript and superscript forms: the font-variant-position property L105:     6. cite85†6.6 Capitalization: the font-variant-caps property L106:     7. cite86†6.7 Numerical formatting: the font-variant-numeric property L107:     8. cite87†6.8 Alternates and swashes: the font-variant-alternates property L108:     9. cite88†6.9 Defining font specific alternates: the @font-feature-values rule L109:       1. cite89†6.9.1 Basic syntax L110:       2. cite90†6.9.2 Multi-valued feature value definitions L111:     10. cite91†6.10 East Asian text rendering: the font-variant-east-asian property L112:     11. cite92†6.11 Overall shorthand for font rendering: the font-variant property L113:     12. cite93†6.12 Low-level font feature settings control: the font-feature-settings property L114:     13. cite94†6.13 Font language override: the font-language-override property L115:   7. cite95†7 Font Feature and Variation Resolution L116:     1. cite96†7.1 Default features L117:     2. cite97†7.2 Feature and variation precedence L118:     3. cite98†7.3 Feature precedence examples L119:   8. cite99†8 Font Variation Properties L120:     1. cite100†8.1 Optical sizing control: the font-optical-sizing property L121:     2. cite101†8.2 Low-level font variation settings control: the font-variation-settings property L122:   9. cite102†9 Color Font Support L123:     1. cite103†9.1 Controlling Color Font Palettes: The font-palette property L124:     2. cite104†9.2 User-defined font color palettes: The @font-palette-values rule L275: This property specifies a prioritized list of font family names or generic family names. A font family defines a set of faces that vary in weight, width or slope. CSS uses the combination of a family name with other font properties to select an individual face. Using this selection mechanism, rather than selecting a face via the style name as is often done in design applications, allows some degree of regularity in textual display when fallback occurs.\nL276: Component values are a comma-separated list indicating alternatives. A user agent iterates through the list of family names until it matches an available font that contains a glyph for the character to be rendered. (See cite77†§ 5.3 Cluster matching .) This allows for differences in available fonts across platforms and for differences in the range of characters supported by individual fonts.\nL277: \nL278:     body {\nL279:       font-family: Helvetica, Verdana, sans-serif;\nL280:     }\nL281: If Helvetica is available, it will be used when rendering. If neither Helvetica nor Verdana is present, then the generic font-family cite357†sans-serif font will be used.\nL282: \nL283: There are three types of font family names, two of which may be used with this property:\nL284: \nL285: cite187†<font-family-name> L286:     The name of a font family, such as Helvetica or Verdana in the previous example.\nL287: \nL288: This might be a locally-instaled font, or might be a web font.\nL289: cite189†<generic-font-family> L290:     Each cite189†<generic-font-family> keyword represents a generic font choice, and behaves as a potential alias for one or more locally-installed fonts belonging to the specified generic font category. A <generic-font-family> can thus be used as a fallback for when an author’s more specific font choices are not available.\nL291: \nL292: There are three types of generic family:\nL293:   1. Generics which apply to all Unicode characters and will always match a locally installed font. For example, cite358†monospace .\nL294: \nL295:   2. Generics which apply to all Unicode characters but may not match to a locally installed font on some systems. For example, cite359†ui-rounded .\nL296: \nL297:   3. Generics which are writing-system specific, only apply to a subset of Unicode characters, and may not match to a locally installed font on some systems. For example, cite360†generic(fangsong) .\nL1693: These descriptors define initial settings that apply when the font defined by an cite979†@font-face rule is rendered. They do not affect font selection. Values are identical to those defined for the corresponding cite848†font-feature-settings and cite855†font-variation-settings properties defined below except that the cite177†CSS-wide keywords are omitted.\nL1694: When multiple font feature descriptors, properties, or variations are used, optionally along with a named instance, the cumulative effect on text rendering is detailed in the section cite95†§ 7 Font Feature and Variation Resolution below.\nL1695: These descriptors set features and variation values on the font object which the cite979†@font-face rule represents, rather than on an entire element. Therefore, when using these descriptors, only some glyphs in an element may be rendered with that feature, due to cite77†§ 5.3 Cluster matching .\nL1696: ### 4.7. Using named instances from variable fonts: the cite1190†font-named-instance descriptor\nL1697: \nL1698: Name:  | font-named-instance\nL1699: For:  | cite979†@font-face L1700: Value:  | auto cite188†| cite365†<string> L1701: Initial:  | auto\nL1702: If the cite1190†font-named-instance descriptor is set to a value other than auto, then the appropriate stage in the cite95†§ 7 Font Feature and Variation Resolution will inspect the font file to find the first named instance in the font which has a localized name equal to the given cite365†<string> according to the rules given in cite75†§ 5.1 Localized name matching . If no such named instance exists, this descriptor is treated as if it has a value of 'font-named-instance/auto'.\nL1703: Otherwise, this named instance’s variation axis values are applied to this position in the cite95†§ 7 Font Feature and Variation Resolution .\nL1704: For example, the following cite979†@font-face block will apply the instance named \"Grotesque\" but will override the \"XHGT\" axis to have a value of 0.7.\nL1705: \nL1706:     @font-face {\nL1707:       font-family: \"AccuroVar\";\nL1708:       src: url(\"accurovar.otf\") format(\"opentype\");\nL1709:       font-named-instance: \"Grotesque\";\nL1710:       font-variation-settings: \"XHGT\" 0.7;\nL1711:     }\nL1712: Note: Because the variation axis values supplied in the cite390†font-weight , cite391†font-width , and cite392†font-style properties are applied before the value in the cite1190†font-named-instance descriptor, there is no need to change the value of those properties when a named instance is desired.\nL1928: The procedure for choosing a font for a given character in a run of text consists of iterating over the font families named by the cite185†font-family property, selecting a font face with the appropriate style based on other font properties and then determining whether a glyph exists for the given character. This is done using the character map of the font, data which maps characters to the default glyph for that character.\nL1929: A font is considered to support a given character if (1) the character is contained in the font’s cite662†character map and (2) if required by the containing script, shaping information is available for that character.\nL1930: Some legacy fonts might include a given character in the cite662†character map but lack the shaping information (e.g. cite1242†OpenType layout tables†www.microsoft.com or cite1243†Graphite tables†scripts.sil.org ) necessary for correctly rendering text runs containing that character.\nL1931: \nL1932: Codepoint sequences consisting of a base character followed by a sequence of combining characters are treated slightly differently, see the section on cite77†cluster matching below.\nL1933: For this procedure, the default font face for a given font family is defined to be the face that would be selected if all font style properties were set to their initial value.\nL1934: \nL1935:   1. Using the computed font property values for a given element, the user agent starts with the first family name specified by the cite185†font-family property.\nL1936:   2. If the family name is a generic family keyword, the user agent looks up the appropriate font family name to be used. User agents may choose the generic font family to use based on the language of the containing element or the Unicode range of the character.\nL1937:   3. For other family names, the user agent attempts to find the family name among fonts defined via cite979†@font-face rules and then among available installed fonts (this may include font aliases), matching names with a cite75†§ 5.1 Localized name matching as outlined in the section above. If the font resources defined for a given face in an @font-face rule are either not available or contain invalid font data, then the face should be treated as not present in the family.\nL1938: If no faces are present for a family defined via @font-face rules, the family should be treated as missing; matching a platform font with the same name must not occur in this case.\nL2082:   * cite1277†first-available-font-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1278†(live test)†wpt.live cite1279†(source)†github.com L2083:   * cite1280†first-available-font-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1281†(live test)†wpt.live cite1282†(source)†github.com L2084:   * cite1283†first-available-font-003.html†wpt.fyi cite1284†(live test)†wpt.live cite1285†(source)†github.com L2085:   * cite1286†first-available-font-004.html†wpt.fyi cite1287†(live test)†wpt.live cite1288†(source)†github.com L2086:   * cite1289†first-available-font-005.html†wpt.fyi cite1290†(live test)†wpt.live cite1291†(source)†github.com L2087:   * cite1292†first-available-font-006.html†wpt.fyi cite1293†(live test)†wpt.live cite1294†(source)†github.com L2088:   * cite1295†first-available-font-007.html†wpt.fyi cite1296†(live test)†wpt.live cite1297†(source)†github.com L2089:   * cite1298†italic-oblique-fallback.html†wpt.fyi cite1299†(live test)†wpt.live cite1300†(source)†github.com L2090: Note: it does not matter whether that font actually has a glyph for the space character.\nL2091: ### 5.3. Cluster matching\nL2092: When text contains characters such as combining marks, ideally the base character should be rendered using the same font as the mark, this assures proper placement of the mark. For this reason, the font matching algorithm for clusters is more specialized than the general case of matching a single character by itself.\nL2093: For sequences containing variation selectors, which indicate the precise glyph to be used for a given character, user agents always attempt cite1301†installed font fallback to find the appropriate glyph before using the default glyph of the base character.\nL2094: A sequence of codepoints containing combining mark or other modifiers is termed a grapheme cluster (see cite1302†[CSS3TEXT] and cite1303†[UAX29] for a more complete description). For a given cluster containing a base character, b and a sequence of combining characters c1, c2…, the entire cluster is matched using these steps:\nL2095:   1. For each family in the font list, a face is chosen using the style selection rules defined in the previous section.\nL2096:     1. If all characters in the sequence b + c1 + c2 … are completely supported by the font, select this font for the sequence.\nL2097:     2. If a sequence of multiple codepoints is canonically equivalent to a single character and the font cite1304†supports that character, select this font for the sequence and use the glyph associated with the canonically equivalent character for the entire cluster.\nL2098:   2. If no font was found in the font list in step 1:\nL2099:     1. If c1 is a variation selector, system fallback must be used to find a font that supports the full sequence of b + c1. If no font on the system cite1304†supports the full sequence, match the single character b using the normal procedure for matching single characters and ignore the variation selector. Note: a sequence with more than one variation selector must be treated as an encoding error and the trailing selectors must be ignored. cite1241†[UNICODE] L3589: This property allows web authors to select whether emoji presentation or text presentation is used for certain emoji code points. Traditionally, these presentation styles were selected by appending Variation Selector 15 (U+FE0E) or Variation Selector 16 (U+FE0F) to certain code points. However, cite181†font-variant-emoji allows web authors to set a default presentation style which can replace the variation selectors.\nL3590: Only the code points cite2106†listed†www.unicode.org by Unicode as contributing to a Unicode emoji presentation sequence are affected by this property. Within this CSS specification, these characters are referred to as Emoji Presentation Participating Code Points. This property has no effect on any other characters.\nL3591: This property is expected to affect font fallback; however, the exact nature of the interaction of font fallback with cite181†font-variant-emoji is explicitly unspecified. However, a variation selector must be included in a previous cluster as defined by the cite77†cluster matching section above. A natural result of this behavior is that a variation selector must not be rendered in a different font than the previous character.\nL3592: It thus causes font fallback to behave as if the respective variation selector was intended (as in the values description); then font fallback, including the cluster fallback rules, is performed, which must take the presentation preference of these \"appended\" variation selectors into account.\nL3593: Even when cite181†font-variant-emoji is used, the presence of Variation Selector 15 (U+FE0E) or Variation Selector 16 (U+FE0F) in the contents of an element override the rendering specified in font-variant-emoji. Therefore, font-variant-emoji sets a default presentation which the text being rendered can opt out of.\nL3594: Note: Different platforms have different conventions about how to handle emoji presentation sequences. A cross-platform UA might wish to follow the conventions of each individual platform, or it might wish to use the same approach on all platforms. When tasked with a request for an emoji style rendering, a UA might wish to disregard fonts which do not include color tables. A different UA might wish instead to use the same mechanical cluster fallback algorithm that it would use for any arbitrary cluster.\nL3595: Variation selectors other than FE0E VARIATION SELECTOR-15 and U+FE0F VARIATION SELECTOR-16 must not have any effect on font selection. If one of these variation selectors is present, but unsupported by the font previously selected, the variation selector is ignored.\nL3596: \nL3597: BCP47’s `-u-` extension to the language tag accepted by `lang` or `xml:lang` should not be considered when the user-agent decides whether to use emoji presentation or text presentation for a particular character.\nL4210:   * Added unicode value to font-variant-emoji\nL4211:   * Removed the media entry from property descriptions\nL4212:   * Broke up font-synthesis into longhands, for future extensibility\nL4213:   * Added override-color descriptor for color palettes\nL4214:   * Defined feature-value-name for font-variant-alternates\nL4215:   * Clarified @font-face src parsing rules\nL4216:   * Allow named instances from variable fonts to be used\nL4217:   * Appendix A: Mapping platform font properties to CSS properties ported over from CSS Fonts 3\nL4218:   * Character handling issues ported over from CSS Fonts 3\nL4219:   * Cluster matching ported over from CSS Fonts 3\nL4220:   * Add new auto value for font-weight, font-style and font-stretch descriptors\nL4221:   * Clarified font-display descriptor value names\nL4222:   * Support named palette entries\nL4223:   * More details on Fang Song generic family\nL4224:   * Changed default angle of oblique fonts to 14deg.\nL4225:   * font-feature-values ported over from CSS Fonts 3\nL4226:   * Clarified handling of unknown or unsupported fragment identifiers\nL4227:   * Clarified that color records in font palettes are zero-indexed\nL4228:   * Consistently use the new generic font families\nL4229:   * Editorial cleanup, clarifying examples, markup improvements, linkfixes, updating to latest references, and so on\nL4230: ### 18.8. Changes from the 20 September 2018 cite2212†CSS Fonts 3 Recommendation†www.w3.org L4231: \nL4232: This summarizes the changes in CSS Fonts 4, compared to CSS Fonts 3.\nL4233:   * Added font-display descriptor for @font-face\nL4234:   * Added @font-feature-values rule\nL4235:   * Added font variation properties, including font-optical-sizing and font-variation-settings\nL4236:   * Added color font support\nL4237:   * Defined feature-value-name for font-variant-alternates\nL4238:   * Added new auto value for font-weight, font-style and font-stretch descriptors\nL4239:   * Added ui-serif, ui-sans-serif, ui-monospace, and ui-rounded generic font families\nL4240:   * Added font-size: xxx-large\nL4241:   * Added font metric override descriptors\nL4242:   * Added some motivation for generic font families\nL4243:   * Added font feature technology keywords opentype, aat, and graphite\nL4244:   * Added math value to font-size\nL4245: ## Conformance\n"
